{"name":"Pac-Man","hex":"#ffe737","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Pac-Man","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Anything that consumes indiscriminately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Pac-Man"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pac-Man","blocks":[{"text":"Pac-Man, originally titled Puck Man in Japan, is a 1980 maze video game developed and published by Namco for arcades. It was released in Japan on May 22, 1980 and by Midway Manufacturing in North America in October 1980. The player controls Pac-Man, who must eat all the dots inside an enclosed maze while avoiding four colored ghosts. Eating large flashing dots called \"Power Pellets\" causes the ghosts to temporarily turn blue and vulnerable, allowing Pac-Man to eat the ghosts for bonus points."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/16/Pac_flyer.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pac-Man","wikidata":"Q173626"},{"title":"Pac-Man (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Pac-Man is an American animated television series produced by Hanna-Barbera Productions and based on the Namco video game franchise of the same title. It premiered on ABC and ran for 44 episodes over two seasons from September 25, 1982, to November 5, 1983. It was the first cartoon based on a video game."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pac-Man_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q2734604"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Pac-Man_gameplay_%281x_pixel-perfect_recreation%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Pac-Man_gameplay_%281x_pixel-perfect_recreation%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"In-game screenshot. The ghosts are chasing Pac-Man. At bottom left is the player's life count, and at bottom right the level icon 